WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
ChatGPT just added brand new voices in six languages that sound more natural and stop the constant checking pauses. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 198. OpenAI released fresh voices for ChatGPT voice, Codex, and dots that handle French, German, Hindi, Mexican Spanish, Korean, and Japanese with clear accents. The voices cut down on those awkward pauses where the system used to say it was checking. Think of the old voice like an old toy robot.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.280
that's stuttered every few words while it's searched for what to say next.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:37.200
The new version works more like a friend who grew up speaking the language

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.780
and keeps the conversation moving without stopping.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.240
Students can now practice Spanish homework out loud and hear the rhythm stay smooth instead of

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:50.700
breaking. Anyone walking the dog or driving can ask questions and get answers that feel like a real

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:56.740
chat rather than a machine struggling. The update already works for every user in the chat GPT app,

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:03.460
Codex and the Dot Agents. Open the chat GPT app on your phone, tap the voice icon, switch

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:08.120
to one of the new languages, and ask it to tell a short story. You will notice right

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:13.800
away how few pauses appear. The change also reaches developers through the API soon so

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:19.040
other apps can use the same smoother speech. Voice mode matters most for people who want to

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.180
use AI without staring at a screen the whole time. Picture the AI as a musician who has

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.980
practice the same song in six different styles until the notes flow without any missed beats.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:33.360
That practice replaces the old habit of stopping to double check each note.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.600
The result turns short voice commands into longer back and forth talks that feel natural.

00:01:39.629 --> 00:01:43.729
You know how your phone keyboard guesses the next word while you text and sometimes finishes

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:44.789
the sentence for you.

00:01:45.369 --> 00:01:48.189
Now picture the same idea but for sound instead of letters.

00:01:48.769 --> 00:01:53.189
The model listens to the tiny slice of audio it just created and predicts the next slice

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:58.369
so the voice stays smooth. It studied real people speaking each language and learned patterns

00:01:58.369 --> 00:02:04.209
like how French questions rise at the end or how Korean keeps a steady rhythm. Those patterns

00:02:04.209 --> 00:02:09.929
let the guesses happen faster and more accurately than before. When the old system paused with checking,

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:15.729
it was basically saying it needed extra time to review its own guess. The updated version has practiced

00:02:15.729 --> 00:02:20.749
enough that it rarely needs that extra step. The outcome feels like the voice is thinking and

00:02:20.749 --> 00:02:23.489
responding instead of stitching together recorded clips.

00:02:23.989 --> 00:02:28.409
The same smooth prediction now works across all six languages, whether you are practicing

00:02:28.409 --> 00:02:30.329
homework or asking for a recipe.

00:02:31.049 --> 00:02:36.089
Next time you hear an AI speak without stumbling, remember it is running an extremely fast version

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:39.109
of autocomplete, but with sound waves instead of letters.

00:02:39.709 --> 00:02:44.289
That single idea explains why the pauses disappeared and the accents improved together.

00:02:45.457 --> 00:02:50.917
BlueSky's Addie AI tool lets you describe a simple game or poll in plain words, and it

00:02:50.917 --> 00:02:52.437
It builds the whole thing instantly.

00:02:52.997 --> 00:02:57.037
You can share the result as its own small website that friends can open right away.

00:02:57.577 --> 00:03:02.697
Go to Blue Sky, find the Adi AI feature, and type something like, make a two-player rock

00:03:02.697 --> 00:03:05.017
paper scissors game with funny animal characters.

00:03:05.617 --> 00:03:09.837
In minutes you will have a working game you can post and play with others without any code.

00:03:10.824 --> 00:03:15.184
Google's Universal Cart tool lets you add items from different stores while searching or

00:03:15.184 --> 00:03:17.964
chatting with Gemini and then check out everything together.

00:03:18.484 --> 00:03:20.404
Open the Gemini app on your phone.

00:03:20.404 --> 00:03:24.264
search for something you want to buy, and look for the cart option that appears

00:03:24.264 --> 00:03:24.924
automatically.

00:03:25.564 --> 00:03:30.144
You can add sneakers from one store and a phone case from another, then see the combined

00:03:30.144 --> 00:03:31.284
total before paying.

00:03:31.884 --> 00:03:36.244
The feature already works across the United States so anyone with the app can test it today.

00:03:36.964 --> 00:03:42.944
And Thropic gave a nonprofit called Metter the same deep access its own team uses, so outsiders

00:03:42.944 --> 00:03:44.524
can check the models for risks.

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:49.484
Metter will publish its own reports and Anthropic agreed not to block those findings.

00:03:49.484 --> 00:03:55.549
YouTube music now adds clear labels to songs made by AI, so listeners know the track did

00:03:55.549 --> 00:03:56.989
not come from a human singer.

00:03:57.629 --> 00:04:01.189
The labels already appear and will soon show up on regular YouTube too.

00:04:01.769 --> 00:04:02.449
And that's a wrap.

00:04:02.809 --> 00:04:05.409
